WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.110
Today you mix chance with functions, roll hundreds of dice in a blink and build a Ludo race.

2
00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:11.710
Put random.randint inside a function and return the result.

3
00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:16.200
Now roll_dice() gives a fresh number every time you call it.

4
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.140
Your game code starts to read like a story.

5
00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.800
A loop can roll a dice hundreds of times in a blink.

6
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:28.330
Counting the results shows patterns, like a six coming up about one time in six.

7
00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:31.850
Scientists and game designers test ideas this way.

8
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:37.320
def take_turn(name, position): handles one player’s whole turn.

9
00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.050
It rolls, moves, prints and returns the new position.

10
00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:44.170
Two players simply call it with different names.

11
00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:53.580
Game rules can be functions too. move(position, roll) returns the square you land on, and keeps you still if you would go past the finish.

12
00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.490
Small rule functions are easy to test.

13
00:00:56.490 --> 00:01:02.240
The parameter sides decides how big the dice is.

14
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.090
Every call returns a new random number, so your output will be different from mine.

15
00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:11.030
This function returns heads or tails, just like the coin toss before a match.

16
00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:14.570
We store the answer and use it in a decision.

17
00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:19.930
The loop rolls the dice six hundred times faster than you can blink.

18
00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:25.250
We get close to one hundred sixes, about one roll in every six.

19
00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:29.010
From square ten, a four takes you to square fourteen.

20
00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:35.100
From square twenty-eight, a five would pass the finish at thirty, so you stay where you are.

21
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.850
The function rolls the dice but forgets to return the number.

22
00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:43.810
So roll holds None, and Python cannot multiply nothing by two.

23
00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.980
Great work! Let’s remember what we learned.

24
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:51.860
Now build along, then try the challenges. You’ve got this!
